# IoT sandbox and whitelist

Every Wi-Fi IoT device, including mesh nodes that only extend coverage, lives on an isolated network. Clients on regular Wi-Fi never join that network and never get a routed or NAT path into it.

## Radio

- Prefer **2.4 GHz** Wi-Fi for range and device compatibility. Throughput is not a design constraint.
- Bluetooth is in scope on the same boundary. Membership rules apply to both radios.

## Who may join

Wi-Fi association and Bluetooth connections accept a whitelist only:

- Controllers deployed to form the mesh
- IoT devices those controllers operate

Phones, laptops, guests, and any other device stay off the whitelist. Do not leave SSID auth, mesh join, or Bluetooth pairing open.

## How people reach devices

The control-panel web app is the only path from regular Wi-Fi into the sandbox. Clients talk to the panel; the panel talks to the sandbox. Do not add NAT, port forwards, or LAN-to-IoT routing so a phone can reach a device directly.

```text
# BAD — bulb on the house SSID, open mesh or BLE pairing,
#       NAT or a route from the LAN into the IoT network
# GOOD — bulb and mesh nodes on the isolated 2.4 GHz network;
#        whitelist = mesh controllers + controlled devices;
#        phone uses the control panel only
```

When changing drivers, host networking, firmware setup, or the panel, keep this boundary. Network layout detail lives in `docs/04-firmware-liberation-and-network-isolation.md`.
